import {icon} from './app.js?v=8';

export function teacher() {
  return `<div class="page-heading"><div><h1>Teacher's corner</h1><p>SCES3274 · Task 1 · Year 4 Science · Photosynthesis</p></div><a href="offline.html" class="btn secondary">${icon('download')} Printable trail</a></div>
  <section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Learning intentions and success criteria</h2>
    <p>For pupils aged about ten. Use partner talk and teacher guidance where needed. The resource focuses on KSSR Science Year 4 learning standards 4.2.1–4.2.4.</p>
    <ol><li>Explain that green plants make their own food through photosynthesis.</li><li>Identify sunlight, water, carbon dioxide and chlorophyll as needs, and starch and oxygen as products.</li><li>Predict and compare results when one condition changes in a simplified model.</li><li>Explain why photosynthesis matters for food, breathing and the balance of gases in the air.</li></ol>
    <p><strong>Success evidence:</strong> a reasoned prediction, experiment log, connected creek paths, environmental changes in Nico's forest, and a written or spoken reflection.</p>
    <p class="caption">The model is a simulation. It does not show actual plant growth rates or replace observing real plants.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Meaningful learning design</h2>
    <p>Pupils begin with a question, test an idea, adjust a model, solve environmental problems and explain what changed. They can revisit activities, discuss evidence with a partner and try again after feedback. The forest connects the Science concept to plants, animals and a shared habitat.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Learning step</th><th>Pupil action</th><th>Evidence to observe</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Wonder</td><td>Predict where Sprout gets food.</td><td>Initial idea and reason.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Explore a leaf</td><td>Watch the story or inspect the playable leaf model.</td><td>Explanation of needs and products.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Test a condition</td><td>Change one input in the leaf lab.</td><td>Prediction, result and revision.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Restore water</td><td>Rotate channels to reach the roots.</td><td>Explanation that water is a need, not food.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Help the forest</td><td>Move objects, restore light and water, and inspect a leaf.</td><td>Science reasoning tied to visible changes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reflect</td><td>Record or speak a new understanding.</td><td>Change from an earlier idea.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Use the activity during a student-centred Science lesson. The SCES3274 macro-teaching task can demonstrate how a teacher questions pupils and responds to their observations.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Curriculum alignment</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Year 4 standard</th><th>Resource evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>4.2.1 Meaning</td><td>Plant mystery, story and leaf model.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4.2.2 Needs</td><td>Four-condition lab, creek rescue and forest light and water puzzles.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4.2.3 Products</td><td>Leaf model shows food formation and oxygen release.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4.2.4 Importance</td><td>Forest pantry, breathing pond and reflection.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Completing an activity or receiving a certificate is participation evidence. It does not automatically award an official DSKP performance level. Use pupil explanations and other classroom evidence for assessment.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="activity-panel teacher-section"><h2>Access and classroom use</h2>
    <ul><li>Keyboard and touch controls, captions, transcripts, optional read-aloud, larger text and reduced motion support different learners.</li><li>The printable trail offers an alternative when devices or connectivity are limited.</li><li>Invite paired discussion and teacher questioning. Give pupils time to revisit a model after feedback.</li><li>No pupil account or full name is needed. Progress and an optional nickname stay on the device.</li></ul>
    <p class="caption">The interactive site needs an HTTP server or hosted connection. Audio depends on the browser's available voices. The forest needs a browser with WebGL 2.</p>
  </section>`;
}

